    {
      "name": "set_site_woo_cart_cache",
      "title": "Set Site Woo Cart Cache",
      "summary": "Enables or disables Woo Cart Caching for a Pressable WordPress site. Woo Cart Caching lets the edge cache keep serving cached pages to WooCommerce shoppers who already have items in their cart, instead of bypassing the cache for every request once a cart exists.",
      "category": "sites"
    },
    {
      "name": "create_site_dns_wizard_link",
      "title": "Create Site DNS Wizard Link",
      "summary": "Issues a DNS Wizard (Entri) share link for a domain, so the person who controls that domain's DNS can point it at a Pressable WordPress site from their own registrar. Reach for this when the domain belongs to someone else; use add_site_domain when Pressable should create the records itself.",
      "category": "sites"
    },
    {
      "name": "list_sites",
      "title": "List Sites",
      "summary": "Lists the WordPress sites on your Pressable account — the sites you own plus the sites you collaborate on. No filter is required, so this is the tool for \"what sites do I have?\".",
      "category": "sites"
    },
    {
      "name": "list_site_wordpress_operations",
      "title": "List Site WordPress Operations",
      "summary": "Lists the WordPress operation history for a Pressable WordPress site (the History feed shown in the control panel), newest first, so a command scheduled with run_site_wpcli_commands can be read back. Covers WP-CLI commands, control-panel bash commands, plugin and theme refreshes and updates, and search-replace runs. Each entry includes the operation id, the background job id, the job type, the status (pending, running, complete, or failed), who ran it, and the operation's captured log, which for a command that SUCCEEDED holds its output inside a \"Command [ <command> ] :::: <output>\" line with that output trimmed to 500 characters, while one that FAILED may instead hold an untrimmed error message in that line, or a bare line such as \"Site is not active.\" with no \"Command [ ... ]\" wrapper. The log is abbreviated here: at most the 20 most recent entries, each capped at 500 bytes taken from the START of the entry (so a long command's echo can crowd out its output), with log_message_count giving the true total and log_messages_truncated saying whether anything was cut — call get_site_wordpress_operation for the fuller log of one operation. Results are paginated (default 20 per page, max 50) and can be filtered by job_type and status; the response also lists the job types present on the site. Commands scheduled with run_site_bash_commands do not create an operation record; their output is recorded in the site activity log instead (call get_site_activity_logs with the action filter \"ssh.command\").",
      "category": "sites"
    },
    {
      "name": "get_site_wordpress_operation",
      "title": "Get Site WordPress Operation",
      "summary": "Retrieves one WordPress operation on a Pressable WordPress site (one entry from the History feed shown in the control panel), including its captured log — the operation's record of the command, not raw unabridged stdout. A command that SUCCEEDED holds its output inside a \"Command [ <command> ] :::: <output>\" line with that output trimmed to 500 characters; a command that FAILED may instead hold an untrimmed error message in that line, or — when it failed before running at all — a bare line such as \"Site is not active.\" with no \"Command [ ... ]\" wrapper to parse. Identify the operation either by job_id (the background job id returned by run_site_wpcli_commands) or by operation_id (from list_site_wordpress_operations); pass exactly one of the two. Returns the job type, the status (pending, running, complete, or failed), who ran it, the queue name and scheduled time, the log (at most the 200 most recent entries, each capped at 10101 bytes — the writers' own per-entry ceiling with their truncation marker included, so an entry a writer already truncated passes through untouched; an entry that still exceeds that (writers can compose two truncated values into one line) keeps its head AND its tail, so the writer's own marker and the size it records survive the cut — with log_message_count and log_messages_truncated reporting what was cut), and timestamps. Poll this after run_site_wpcli_commands to read a command's result. Commands scheduled with run_site_bash_commands do not create an operation record; their output is recorded in the site activity log instead (call get_site_activity_logs with the action filter \"ssh.command\").",
      "category": "sites"
    },
    {
      "name": "get_site_file_modification_settings",
      "title": "Get Site File Modification Settings",
      "summary": "Reads the WordPress file-modification wp-config constants for a Pressable site. DISALLOW_FILE_EDIT hides the built-in plugin and theme code editor in the WordPress admin; DISALLOW_FILE_MODS additionally blocks plugin and theme installs, updates, and deletions. Returns the persisted state of both constants.",
      "category": "sites"
    },
    {
      "name": "update_site_file_modification_constant",
      "title": "Update Site File Modification Constant",
      "summary": "Sets or removes a WordPress file-modification constant in a Pressable site's wp-config.php. DISALLOW_FILE_EDIT hides the built-in plugin and theme code editor in the WordPress admin; DISALLOW_FILE_MODS additionally blocks plugin and theme installs, updates, and deletions. Enabling defines the constant as true; disabling removes it. The site must be active. Returns the persisted state of both constants.",
      "category": "sites"
    },
    {
      "name": "get_account_details",
      "title": "Get Account Details",
      "summary": "Returns the authenticated account's full overview: contact details (name, organization, email, phone number), affiliate status, the name of the product the account is subscribed to, the default PHP version and datacenter applied to new sites, how many sites the account owner is a collaborator on, and site capacity broken down into billable and staging allowances. product_name is the plan the account pays for, resolved for hybrid plans as well as single-tier ones, and is NOT the plan_type returned by get_account_site_counts, which only says whether the plan is single-tier or hybrid. It is null when the account has no subscription, and when a hybrid plan has no product attached. Use get_account_site_counts when all you need is billable capacity before creating a site; use this tool for the full account overview, including staging capacity.",
      "category": "accounts"
    },
    {
      "name": "add_dns_mail_provider_records",
      "title": "Add DNS Mail Provider Records",
      "summary": "Adds a mail provider's recommended DNS records to a zone in one call. Supported providers: gsuite (Google Workspace), office365, zoho, namecheap, namecom (Name.com), and proton. Each provider adds its own bundle of MX records plus, depending on the provider, SPF TXT, CNAME, and SRV records. Refuses outright when the zone apex already carries MX or SPF records, so it will not stack a second mail provider on top of an existing one. It is otherwise not idempotent: if some other record in the bundle already exists the call fails part-way and the records written before the conflict are kept. Use list_dns_zones to discover zone IDs and list_dns_zone_records to review the zone first. Returns every record present in the zone after the call that was not present before it.",
      "category": "accounts"
    },
    {
      "name": "get_site_git_configuration",
      "title": "Get Site Git Configuration",
      "summary": "Reads the GitHub deployment configuration for a Pressable site. Returns the connected repository URL, the deploy branch, the destination path under htdocs, the repository subdirectory that path is deployed from, whether the site is connected to git, and whether a GitHub access token is stored. The token itself is never returned. The site must be opted in to the new GitHub integration.",
      "category": "sites"
    },
    {
      "name": "connect_site_git_repository",
      "title": "Connect Site Git Repository",
      "summary": "Connects a Pressable site to a GitHub repository for push deployments and registers the push webhook on that repository. Requires a GitHub access token already stored for the account, and a site that is not yet connected — use update_site_git_configuration to change an existing connection. Connecting stores the configuration only; it does not deploy. Trigger the first deploy from the Pressable dashboard or POST /v1/sites/:site_id/git/deploy. The site must be opted in to the new GitHub integration.",
      "category": "sites"
    },
    {
